<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Calusa</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 CE – 1760 CE (approx.) · capital Calos (Mound Key)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A powerful chiefdom of south-western Florida, unusual in building a complex, tribute-taking society on fishing rather than farming, which resisted Spain for two centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Calusa grew out of the fishing and shell-gathering cultures of the Gulf coast estuaries around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlotte-harbor">Charlotte Harbor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estero-bay">Estero Bay</a>. By around 1000 they were building large shell mounds, ridges and canals, and their capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mound-key">Calos</a>, on Mound Key, became the centre of a paramount chiefdom that dominated much of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> agriculture, the Calusa lived on fish, shellfish and plants from rich estuaries, yet supported a hereditary paramount chief, priests and warriors. Subject towns, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tequesta">Tequesta</a> and others around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-okeechobee">Lake Okeechobee</a>, sent tribute. They dug canoe canals, notably across Pine Island, and carved fine wooden masks and figures like those found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/key-marco">Key Marco</a>. In 1566 the chief known to the Spanish as Carlos met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-menendez-de-aviles">Pedro Menéndez de Avilés</a>, who founded a short-lived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> mission.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Calusa wounded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-ponce-de-leon">Juan Ponce de León</a> fatally in 1521 and kept Spanish missions out. Epidemics and, after 1700, slave raids by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamasee">Yamasee</a> allies of English <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-carolina">Carolina</a> destroyed them. Survivors fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida-keys">Keys</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, the last leaving in 1760.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/calusa.jpg" alt="Calusa" width="320" height="280"><figcaption>Image: KiwiNova · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calusa-Chiefdom-Map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 CE – 1760 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Calos (Mound Key)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Carlos, Felipe</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Calusa traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Calusa</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Calusa chiefdom, Calusa kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1380&amp;focus=calusa" aria-label="Calusa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2273 762 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Calusa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2593 1018h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M2593 1018h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day United States. <a href="/atlas?year=1380&amp;focus=calusa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

